from afar these mountains in the french  alps seem picture-perfect but up close  scientists are finding more of this  red patches known as snow blood  the color is actually microscopic algae  scientists suspect it turns red to  protect itself from intense sun rays  when it's in the snow it accumulates a  pigment like sunscreen to protect itself  from the light intensity it's the same  concept as people going skiing and  putting on sunscreen so fundamentally  the algae is still green but it's hidden  behind a big shield of red pigment  about 2  500 meters above sea level a team of  experts is collecting samples to see if  climate change is fueling snow blood  researchers say it appears to be part of  a vicious cycle  when the level of carbon dioxide  increases in the atmosphere we find red  snow more often it's likely linked to  global warming and the warmer it is the  more algae appears and the more algae  the faster the snow melts  snow levels are dropping as a result of  rising temperatures and the alps have  been hit disproportionately hard  locals who are out in the mountains tell  us they're seeing these formations of  red snow in the early summer some of the  patches can be subtle but others can be  extremely strong  if it continues scientists say excessive  snow melt in the alps has the potential  to affect entire ecosystems far beyond  its mountains  katie lopez  al jazeera
 

